          "question": "I have reflux and I also take ibuprofen for my knees.",
          "answer": "I could not find passages in my reference material that explain how ibuprofen affects reflux or the stomach, so I can't describe those effects from my sources. I can share what I did find, and some general suggestions.\n\n**What the sources say:** The iron-deficiency anemia reference lists anti-inflammatory medicines among the things a clinician may check when looking for the cause of low iron. It does not say how these medicines work in the stomach. Ibuprofen belongs to this drug group, but the passage does not name it.\n\n**What to discuss with your clinician or pharmacist:** Tell them you have reflux and take ibuprofen for your knees, and ask whether ibuprofen is still right for you. Ask whether other options or extra stomach protection would suit you. They can weigh your full medical history, which I cannot do.\n\n**Get urgent help now if you notice:**\n- Vomiting blood, or vomit that looks like coffee grounds\n- Black, tarry, foul-smelling stools\n- Large amounts of blood from the back passage, or bleeding that keeps going\n- Bleeding along with dizziness, fainting, a racing heart, cold clammy skin, confusion, or breathlessness\n- Sudden or severe stomach pain, especially if it is rigid or comes on within minutes\n\nCall emergency services for any of these. Black stools can look harmless, so treat them as bleeding until a clinician has checked.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
